\section{Octonionic Setup and the \texorpdfstring{$\mathbb{C}$}{C}-Projection Lemma}
\label{app:setup}

This appendix fixes the octonionic conventions used throughout --- the multiplication table, the
preferred complex structure, and the embedding of the qubit amplitudes --- and proves the
$\mathbb{C}$-projection lemma stated as Lemma~\ref{lem:cproj} in the main text.

\subsection{Octonions: the multiplication table}

We construct $\mathbb{O}$ by Cayley--Dickson doubling: pairs of quaternions with
$(a,b)(c,d) = (ac - \bar d b,\; da + b\bar c)$ and $\overline{(a,b)} = (\bar a, -b)$, the quaternions
built from pairs of complex numbers, and so on down to $\mathbb{R}$ \autocite{baez2002octonions}.
With the basis ordering $e_0 = 1, e_1, \dots, e_7$ this fixes the multiplication completely; the
products of imaginary units are encoded by seven ordered triples, cyclic in the sense that
$(i,j,k)$ means $e_ie_j = e_k$, $e_je_k = e_i$, $e_ke_i = e_j$:
\begin{equation}
  (1,2,3),\quad (1,4,5),\quad (1,7,6),\quad (2,4,6),\quad (2,5,7),\quad (3,4,7),\quad (3,6,5).
  \label{eq:fano-lines}
\end{equation}
Note the orientations: in particular $e_1e_7 = e_6$ and hence $e_6e_7 = -e_1$, with the third line
running $(1,7,6)$ rather than $(1,6,7)$. This detail matters below. Conjugation is
$\bar o = x_0 - \sum_{i\ge1}x_ie_i$, the norm is $|o|^2 = o\bar o$, and the single structural fact
used throughout is the composition identity
\begin{equation}
  |ab|^2 = |a|^2\,|b|^2 \qquad \text{for all } a,b\in\mathbb{O},
  \label{eq:composition}
\end{equation}
which holds for $\mathbb{R},\mathbb{C},\mathbb{H},\mathbb{O}$ and no larger Cayley--Dickson algebra.

\subsection{The computability split and the complex structure}

Fix $e_1$ as the preferred complex direction, selecting
$\mathbb{C} = \operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{R}}\{e_0,e_1\}$ and the decomposition
$\mathbb{O}\cong\mathbb{C}\oplus\mathbb{C}^3$ with
$\mathbb{C}^3 = \operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{R}}\{e_2,\dots,e_7\}$; the stabiliser of $e_1$ in
$G_2 = \operatorname{Aut}(\mathbb{O})$ is the $SU(3)$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:gsm}. The complex
structure on $\mathbb{O}$ is \emph{left multiplication by $e_1$},
\begin{equation}
  J := L_{e_1},\qquad J^2 = -\mathbb{1},
\end{equation}
which acts on the basis, by the table~\eqref{eq:fano-lines}, as
\begin{equation}
  J(e_0) = e_1,\qquad J(e_2) = e_3,\qquad J(e_4) = e_5,\qquad J(e_6) = -e_7.
  \label{eq:Jaction}
\end{equation}
$J$ preserves the four real planes
$\{e_0,e_1\},\{e_2,e_3\},\{e_4,e_5\},\{e_6,e_7\}$ --- the \emph{blocks} --- because these are
exactly the pairs whose units multiply into $\pm e_1$: the pair $\{0,1\}$ trivially, and the pairs
$\{2,3\}, \{4,5\},\\ \{6,7\}$ because $(1,2,3)$, $(1,4,5)$ and $(1,7,6)$ are the three Fano lines through
$e_1$. Every imaginary direction spans a commutative plane with $e_0$; what is special about $e_1$ is
this block structure and, below, its alignment with the qubit amplitudes.

\subsection{The identification of \texorpdfstring{$\mathbb{C}^4$}{C4} with \texorpdfstring{$\mathbb{O}$}{O}}

A three-qubit state $\psi\in\mathbb{C}^8$ with components $\psi_{ijk}$ splits on the first qubit into
$\psi_0 = (\psi_{000},\psi_{001},\psi_{010},\\ \psi_{011})$ and
$\psi_1 = (\psi_{100},\psi_{101},\psi_{110},\psi_{111})$ in $\mathbb{C}^4$. Each is identified with
an octonion by sending the $k$-th complex amplitude $c_k = x_k + \mathrm{i}y_k$ into the $k$-th
block, with the imaginary part along $J(e_{2k})$:
\begin{equation}
  c_0,c_1,c_2,c_3 \;\longmapsto\;
  x_0e_0 + y_0e_1 \;+\; x_1e_2 + y_1e_3 \;+\; x_2e_4 + y_2e_5 \;+\; x_3e_6 \,\boldsymbol{-}\, y_3e_7,
  \label{eq:embedding}
\end{equation}
the sign on the last term forced by $J(e_6) = -e_7$ in~\eqref{eq:Jaction}. By construction the
multiplication-by-$\mathrm{i}$ of the qubit amplitudes coincides with $J$, so the embedding is
$\mathbb{C}$-linear. We emphasise that the sign is not optional: the uniform prescription
``imaginary part $\to +e_{2k+1}$'' is $J$-adapted on the first three blocks but not the fourth, and
with it the lemma below fails. (Both statements have been verified numerically to machine precision,
alongside every identity in these appendices.)

Writing $\mathcal{C}:\psi\mapsto(o_1,o_2)$ for the resulting map, the octonionic Hopf map is
$h(o_1,o_2) = (2\,o_1\bar o_2,\,\\ |o_1|^2-|o_2|^2)$; we study $\mathbf{h} := o_1\bar o_2$ and its
$\mathbb{C}$-projection $\pi_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbf{h}) := h_0 + \mathrm{i}\,h_1$, read as a complex
number.

\subsection{Proof of the \texorpdfstring{$\mathbb{C}$}{C}-projection lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:cproj}]
Write $o_1 = \sum_a u_ae_a$, $o_2 = \sum_b v_be_b$. By bilinearity,
$\mathbf{h} = \sum_{a,b}u_av_b\,e_a\bar e_b$, and a term lands in
$\operatorname{span}\{e_0,e_1\}$ exactly when $a$ and $b$ lie in the \emph{same block}: $a=b$ gives
$e_a\bar e_a = 1$, and distinct same-block pairs give $\pm e_1$ by~\eqref{eq:fano-lines}; any
cross-block pair multiplies into one of $e_2,\dots,e_7$ (the Fano lines not through $e_1$) and
contributes only to $\mathbb{C}^3$.

The bookkeeping, block by block, with $(o_1)_k = x_k+\mathrm{i}y_k$ and $(o_2)_k = x'_k+\mathrm{i}y'_k$
the complex amplitudes recovered through~\eqref{eq:embedding}:

\emph{Diagonal terms.} $e_a\bar e_a = 1$ for every $a$, so the $e_0$ component is
$\sum_a u_av_a = \sum_k (x_kx'_k + y_ky'_k) = \sum_k\operatorname{Re}\bigl(\overline{(o_1)_k}(o_2)_k\bigr)$,
the signs on the fourth block squaring away.

\emph{Block $\{0,1\}$.} $e_0\bar e_1 = -e_1$ and $e_1\bar e_0 = e_1$, contributing
$(u_1v_0 - u_0v_1)e_1 = (y_0x'_0 - x_0y'_0)e_1
= -\operatorname{Im}\bigl(\overline{(o_1)_0}(o_2)_0\bigr)e_1$.

\emph{Blocks $\{2,3\}$ and $\{4,5\}$.} From $(1,2,3)$: $e_2\bar e_3 = -e_2e_3 = -e_1$ and
$e_3\bar e_2 = e_2e_3 = e_1$, contributing $(u_3v_2-u_2v_3)e_1 =
-\operatorname{Im}\bigl(\overline{(o_1)_1}(o_2)_1\bigr)e_1$; identically for $\{4,5\}$ via $(1,4,5)$.

\emph{Block $\{6,7\}$ --- the reversed line.} From $(1,7,6)$: $e_6e_7 = -e_1$, so
$e_6\bar e_7 = +e_1$ and $e_7\bar e_6 = -e_1$, contributing $(u_6v_7 - u_7v_6)e_1$. With the adapted
embedding~\eqref{eq:embedding}, $u_6 = x_3$, $u_7 = -y_3$ and likewise for $v$, so
$u_6v_7 - u_7v_6 = -x_3y'_3 + y_3x'_3 = -\operatorname{Im}\bigl(\overline{(o_1)_3}(o_2)_3\bigr)$:
the reversed Fano orientation and the adapted sign cancel, and the block contributes with the same
form as the others. (With the unadapted sign this block would contribute
$+\operatorname{Im}(\cdots)$, breaking the identity --- this is the failure noted above.)

Summing,
\begin{equation}
  \pi_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbf{h})
  = \sum_{k=0}^{3}\operatorname{Re}\bigl(\overline{(o_1)_k}(o_2)_k\bigr)
  - \mathrm{i}\sum_{k=0}^{3}\operatorname{Im}\bigl(\overline{(o_1)_k}(o_2)_k\bigr)
  = \overline{\langle o_1,o_2\rangle_{\mathbb{C}^4}},
\end{equation}
the conjugate of the standard Hermitian inner product. In particular
$\pi_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbf{h}) = 0$ if and only if the branches are $\mathbb{C}^4$-orthogonal.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{app:cor:cproj}
By~\eqref{eq:composition}, $|\mathbf{h}|^2 = |o_1|^2|o_2|^2$, so the $\mathbb{C}$-fraction
$C_{\mathrm{frac}}(\psi) = |\pi_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbf{h})|^2/|\mathbf{h}|^2$ is a well-defined
local invariant wherever $\mathbf{h}\neq0$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The base sphere in physical coordinates, and the ceiling lemma}

Write the full normalised Hopf image as
\begin{equation}
  H(\psi) = \bigl(2\,\mathbf{h},\;x_9\bigr)\in\mathbb{O}\oplus\mathbb{R},
  \qquad x_9 := |o_1|^2-|o_2|^2,
\end{equation}
so that $|H|^2 = 4|o_1|^2|o_2|^2 + (|o_1|^2-|o_2|^2)^2 = (|o_1|^2+|o_2|^2)^2 = 1$: the image lies on
the base $S^8$. Its nine coordinates organise physically.

\begin{lemma}[The base is Bloch $\oplus$ colour]\label{app:lem:base}
Let $\rho = \tfrac12(\mathbb{1}+\vec r\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma})$ be the split qubit's reduced state,
with Bloch vector $\vec r$. Then $x_9 = r_z$ and $2\,\pi_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbf{h}) = r_x -
\mathrm{i}\,r_y$ exactly, hence
\begin{equation}
  r^2 = x_9^2 + |2\pi_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbf{h})|^2,
  \qquad
  r^2 + |w|^2 = 1,
  \label{eq:base-split}
\end{equation}
where $w\in\mathbb{C}^3$ is the colour component of $2\mathbf{h}$. The base $S^8$ is the unit sphere
of $(\text{Bloch vector of the cut}) \\ \oplus(\text{colour sector})$: three real dimensions of
single-qubit data and six of colour.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
In the branch basis, $\rho_{00} = |o_1|^2$, $\rho_{11} = |o_2|^2$ and
$\rho_{01} = \langle\varphi_1,\varphi_0\rangle = \overline{\langle o_1,o_2\rangle} =
\pi_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbf{h})$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:cproj}. Comparing with
$\rho_{00}-\rho_{11} = r_z$ and $\rho_{01} = \tfrac12(r_x-\mathrm{i}r_y)$ gives the identifications;
the second identity in~\eqref{eq:base-split} is $|H|^2 = 1$ with $|2\mathbf{h}|^2 =
|2\pi_{\mathbb{C}}|^2 + |w|^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Ceiling lemma]\label{app:lem:ceiling}
For any three-qubit state and any split,
\begin{equation}
  \sup_{U_1}\,C_{\mathrm{frac}} = r^2,
\end{equation}
the squared Bloch radius of that cut's marginal; the supremum is attained exactly when $U_1$ rotates
the Bloch vector into the equatorial plane $x_9 = 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{app:lem:base} and $|2\mathbf{h}|^2 = 1-x_9^2$,
\begin{equation}
  C_{\mathrm{frac}} = \frac{|2\pi_{\mathbb{C}}|^2}{|2\mathbf{h}|^2}
  = \frac{r^2 - x_9^2}{1 - x_9^2},
\end{equation}
which is decreasing in $x_9^2$ (its derivative in $x_9^2$ is $(r^2-1)/(1-x_9^2)^2 < 0$). A
first-qubit unitary conjugates $\rho$, rotating $\vec r$ freely at fixed length, so $x_9$ ranges over
$[-r,r]$, and the maximum $r^2$ is attained at $x_9 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Instances]\label{app:cor:instances}
(i) On the canonical GHZ orbit $r = 0$ for every cut, so the $\mathbb{C}$-fraction vanishes
identically: confinement. (ii) On the canonical W orbit $r = \tfrac13$, so
$\sup C_{\mathrm{frac}} = \tfrac19$. (iii) For the tilted states
$\alpha\lvert000\rangle+\beta\lvert111\rangle$, $r = |\alpha|^2-|\beta|^2$ and the orbit ceiling is
$(|\alpha|^2-|\beta|^2)^2$.
\end{corollary}

\section{GHZ Confinement and Its Characterisation}
\label{app:ghz-proof}

We prove Proposition~\ref{prop:ghz} in both directions: the $\mathbb{C}$-projection vanishes
identically on the local-unitary orbit of
$\lvert\mathrm{GHZ}\rangle = (\lvert000\rangle+\lvert111\rangle)/\sqrt2$ under every split, and this
property characterises that orbit exactly. The scope deserves emphasis at the outset: the
characterisation selects the \emph{local-unitary orbit} of the maximally entangled representative,
not the GHZ SLOCC class, which is strictly larger; the distinction, and its physical reading, are
the subject of \S\ref{sec:canonical-orbits} in the main text.

\subsection{The forward direction}

\begin{proof}[Proof, forward direction]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:cproj} it suffices to show $\langle o_1,o_2\rangle_{\mathbb{C}^4} = 0$ for every
local-unitary image, under each split.

\emph{Initial data.} Splitting the bare state on the first qubit gives
$\psi_0 = \tfrac1{\sqrt2}(1,0,0,0)$ and $\psi_1 = \tfrac1{\sqrt2}(0,0,0,1)$: orthogonal, with equal
norms $\tfrac12$.

\emph{Internal unitaries.} $U_2\otimes U_3$ acts unitarily on the $\mathbb{C}^4$ encoding qubits two
and three, so $\varphi_0 = (U_2\otimes U_3)\psi_0$ and $\varphi_1 = (U_2\otimes U_3)\psi_1$ retain
orthogonality and equal norms.

\emph{First-qubit mixing.} With
$U_1 = \begin{psmallmatrix} a & b\\ -\bar b & \bar a\end{psmallmatrix}$, $|a|^2+|b|^2 = 1$, the
branches become $o_1 = a\varphi_0+b\varphi_1$, $o_2 = -\bar b\varphi_0+\bar a\varphi_1$, and,
conjugate-linear in the first argument,
\begin{align}
  \langle o_1,o_2\rangle
  &= \bar a\bar b\bigl(|\varphi_1|^2-|\varphi_0|^2\bigr)
   + \bar a^2\langle\varphi_0,\varphi_1\rangle
   - \bar b^2\langle\varphi_1,\varphi_0\rangle.
  \label{eq:general-ip}
\end{align}
With orthogonal, equal-norm branches every term vanishes, for all $U_1$. (Commutativity of
$\mathbb{C}$ is also load-bearing: the first term arises as
$-\bar a\bar b\,|\varphi_0|^2 + \bar b\bar a\,|\varphi_1|^2$, and combining it requires
$\bar a\bar b = \bar b\bar a$.)

\emph{Symmetry across splits.} The only facts used are that the split qubit's reduced state is
maximally mixed; for $\lvert\mathrm{GHZ}\rangle$ all three are $\tfrac12\mathbb{1}$, and local
unitaries preserve reduced spectra, so the argument runs identically for each split, each with its
own identification~\eqref{eq:embedding} of that split's $\mathbb{C}^4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The colour equator]\label{app:cor:s5}
On the canonical GHZ orbit, $\vec r = 0$ for every cut, so by Lemma~\ref{app:lem:base} the
normalised Hopf image has $x_9 = 0$, $\pi_{\mathbb{C}}(\mathbf{h}) = 0$ and $|w| = 1$: it lies on
the equatorial $S^5\subset S^8$, the unit sphere of the colour sector $\mathbb{C}^3$. (Numerically
the image ranges over the whole of this sphere; we record the coverage as observed rather than
proved.)
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The characterisation}

\begin{theorem}[Characterisation of the GHZ orbit]\label{app:thm:char}
For a pure three-qubit state $\psi$, the following are equivalent:
(i) the $\mathbb{C}$-fraction vanishes for every local unitary and every split;
(ii) all three single-qubit reduced states are maximally mixed;
(iii) $\psi$ is local-unitarily equivalent to $\lvert\mathrm{GHZ}\rangle$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{(i)$\Leftrightarrow$(ii).} Immediate from Lemma~\ref{app:lem:ceiling}: for each split,
$\sup_{U_1}C_{\mathrm{frac}} = r^2$, so the $\mathbb{C}$-fraction vanishes for every local unitary
exactly when $r = 0$, i.e.\ when that cut's marginal is maximally mixed; the internal unitaries
change neither $r$ nor the conclusion, and requiring this under all three splits gives (ii). The
orbit-wide quantifier is essential: at a \emph{fixed} $U_1$ the single equation
$\langle o_1,o_2\rangle = 0$ does not force maximal mixing --- the biseparable state
$\alpha\lvert000\rangle+\beta\lvert101\rangle$ and the tilted state
$\alpha\lvert000\rangle+\beta\lvert111\rangle$ both have $C_{\mathrm{frac}} = 0$ at $U_1=\mathbb{1}$
with $\rho_1\neq\tfrac12\mathbb{1}$ when $|\alpha|\neq|\beta|$, their orbit ceilings being
$(|\alpha|^2-|\beta|^2)^2 > 0$.

\emph{(ii)$\Rightarrow$(iii).} Every pure three-qubit state is local-unitarily equivalent to the
canonical form
$\lambda_0\lvert000\rangle + \lambda_1e^{\mathrm{i}\phi}\lvert100\rangle + \lambda_2\lvert101\rangle
+ \lambda_3\lvert110\rangle + \lambda_4\lvert111\rangle$ with $\lambda_i\ge0$
\autocite{acin2000generalized}. The first-qubit branches are $\varphi_0 = (\lambda_0,0,0,0)$ and
$\varphi_1 = (\lambda_1e^{\mathrm{i}\phi},\lambda_2,\lambda_3,\lambda_4)$, so
$\rho_1 = \tfrac12\mathbb{1}$ forces $\lambda_0^2 = \tfrac12$ and
$\langle\varphi_0,\varphi_1\rangle = \lambda_0\lambda_1e^{\mathrm{i}\phi} = 0$, hence
$\lambda_1 = 0$. The second-qubit branch with that qubit in $\lvert0\rangle$ has squared norm
$\lambda_0^2+\lambda_2^2$, so $\rho_2 = \tfrac12\mathbb{1}$ forces $\lambda_2 = 0$; likewise
$\rho_3 = \tfrac12\mathbb{1}$ forces $\lambda_3 = 0$. Then $\lambda_4^2 = \tfrac12$ and
$\psi \simeq (\lvert000\rangle+\lvert111\rangle)/\sqrt2$.

\emph{(iii)$\Rightarrow$(i)} is the forward direction. Note that no hypothesis of genuine
tripartiteness is needed: condition (ii) alone excludes products and biseparable states.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Orbit, not class]
The GHZ SLOCC class additionally contains the tilted states
$\alpha\lvert000\rangle+\beta\lvert111\rangle$, $\alpha\neq\beta$, which are genuinely tripartite
(non-zero three-tangle) but whose orbits acquire a non-zero $\mathbb{C}$-projection --- numerically
reaching values well above the W-orbit ceiling of $\tfrac19$. The confinement statement is therefore
a property of the canonical maximally entangled orbit, and the identification of that orbit as the
physical particle configuration is made, and discussed, in \S\ref{sec:canonical-orbits}.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Remark: what singles out the preferred direction}

The proof used two features of $e_1$. First, commutativity --- the cancellation in
\eqref{eq:general-ip} needs $\bar a\bar b = \bar b\bar a$ --- but this alone selects nothing, since
every imaginary unit spans a commutative plane with $e_0$. What is unique to $e_1$ is
\emph{alignment}: its three Fano lines~\eqref{eq:fano-lines} partition the remaining units into the
blocks $\{2,3\},\{4,5\},\{6,7\}$, and these coincide (after the $J$-adaptation
of~\eqref{eq:embedding}) with the real--imaginary pairing of the qubit amplitudes. For any other
imaginary direction the corresponding blocks pair coordinates of \emph{different} qubit amplitudes,
the resulting bilinear form is not the Hermitian inner product of the qubit $\mathbb{C}^4$, and the
orbit-wide cancellation fails. Concretely: for the bare representative the Hopf image lies along
$e_6$ (on the line $(1,7,6)$ through the preferred direction), and on the orbit the components along
$e_0$ and $e_1$ vanish identically --- that is the theorem --- while the components along every one
of $e_2,\dots,e_7$ are generically non-zero. The confinement result is thus tied specifically to the
computability split, not to an arbitrary choice of complex direction.

\section{W Accessibility and the Distinguished \texorpdfstring{$T_3$}{T3} Axis}
\label{app:w-proof}

\subsection{Accessibility}

We prove Proposition~\ref{prop:w-access}: on the local-unitary orbit of
$\lvert\mathrm{W}\rangle = (\lvert001\rangle+\lvert010\rangle+\lvert100\rangle)/\sqrt3$, the
$\mathbb{C}$-projection is generically non-zero, vanishing only on a measure-zero subset, with
$\sup C_{\mathrm{frac}} = \tfrac19$.

\begin{proof}
Splitting on the first qubit, $\psi_0 = \tfrac1{\sqrt3}(0,1,1,0)$ and
$\psi_1 = \tfrac1{\sqrt3}(1,0,0,0)$ are orthogonal with \emph{unequal} norms
$|\psi_0|^2 = \tfrac23$, $|\psi_1|^2 = \tfrac13$. Orthogonality kills the $\bar a^2,\bar b^2$ terms
of~\eqref{eq:general-ip}, leaving
\begin{equation}
  \langle o_1,o_2\rangle = \bar a\bar b\bigl(\tfrac13-\tfrac23\bigr) = -\tfrac{\bar a\bar b}{3},
\end{equation}
which vanishes only when $ab = 0$ --- $U_1$ diagonal or anti-diagonal, a measure-zero subset of
$SU(2)$, on which the bare representative itself sits. With $p = |a|^2$:
$|o_1|^2 = (1+p)/3$, $|o_2|^2 = (2-p)/3$, $|\langle o_1,o_2\rangle|^2 = p(1-p)/9$, so
by~\eqref{eq:composition}
\begin{equation}
  C_{\mathrm{frac}} = \frac{p(1-p)}{(1+p)(2-p)},
  \label{eq:wcfrac}
\end{equation}
maximised at $p = \tfrac12$ with value $\tfrac19$. The reduced spectrum $(\tfrac23,\tfrac13)$ is a
local-unitary invariant, so the conclusion holds orbit-wide, and by the permutation symmetry of
$\lvert\mathrm{W}\rangle$, under every split.
\end{proof}

The value $\tfrac19$ is the instance $r = \tfrac13$ of Lemma~\ref{app:lem:ceiling} --- the square of
the marginal's Bloch radius --- attained exactly when $U_1$ rotates that Bloch vector into the
equatorial plane. The geometry is sharper than a latitude band. By~\eqref{eq:base-split} with
$r = \tfrac13$ fixed on the orbit, the colour-component norm is \emph{constant},
$|w| = \sqrt{8/9}$: the W orbit's Hopf image lies on the product of a colour five-sphere of radius
$\sqrt{8/9}$ with a Bloch two-sphere of radius $\tfrac13$, and the latitude band
$|x_9|\le\tfrac13$ is merely this locus's shadow on the $x_9$ axis. In particular the image is
\emph{disjoint} from the colour equator $S^5$ of Corollary~\ref{app:cor:s5}, which has $|w| = 1$:
even where the W orbit crosses the equatorial plane $x_9 = 0$, its colour radius remains
$\sqrt{8/9} < 1$, the missing $\tfrac19$ of squared norm sitting entirely in the $\mathbb{C}$-plane
--- which is exactly where the $\mathbb{C}$-fraction attains its ceiling. The two canonical orbits
thus occupy disjoint compact loci on the base, the geometric form of the statement that no change of
local frame connects them.

\subsection{The doublet: a distinguished axis from a non-degenerate spectrum}

We prove Proposition~\ref{prop:w-doublet} in its corrected form: each single-qubit reduced state on
the W orbit has the non-degenerate spectrum $(\tfrac23,\tfrac13)$, whose eigenbasis singles out a
$T_3$ axis --- the structure of a weak-isospin doublet --- which the degenerate GHZ marginal lacks.

\begin{proof}
Group $\lvert\mathrm{W}\rangle = \tfrac1{\sqrt3}\bigl[\lvert0\rangle(\lvert01\rangle+\lvert10\rangle)
+\lvert1\rangle\lvert00\rangle\bigr]$ and trace qubits two and three. The cross terms vanish since
$\langle00\vert(\lvert01\rangle+\lvert10\rangle) = 0$, giving
\begin{equation}
  \rho_1 = \begin{pmatrix}\tfrac23 & 0\\ 0 & \tfrac13\end{pmatrix}
         = \tfrac12\mathbb{1} + \tfrac16\,\hat n\!\cdot\!\boldsymbol{\sigma},
\end{equation}
a Bloch vector of length $\tfrac13$ along $\hat n = \hat z$; by the permutation symmetry of
$\lvert\mathrm{W}\rangle$ the same holds for $\rho_2,\rho_3$.

The invariant content is the non-degeneracy. A non-degenerate spectrum determines a unique
eigenbasis, hence a distinguished axis $\hat n$ and an ordered pair of eigenstates --- the two
doublet members, with $T_3 = \pm\tfrac12$ assigned along $\hat n$ --- and the marginal's symmetry is
broken from $SU(2)$ to the $U(1)$ of rotations about $\hat n$. Under a local unitary the state is
conjugated, the Bloch vector rotating covariantly (the traceless part transforms in the adjoint),
so the \emph{existence} of the distinguished axis is orbit-wide even as its direction moves; the
$SU(2)$ raising and lowering operators with respect to $\hat n$ map the two eigenstates into one
another, which is the doublet action. The GHZ marginal $\tfrac12\mathbb{1}$, by contrast, is
invariant under all of $SU(2)$: it determines no axis and no doublet pair. The eigenvalues
$(\tfrac23,\tfrac13)$ are local-unitary invariants, so all statements hold across the orbit.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bipartite shadow]\label{app:cor:bell}
Tracing one qubit from $\lvert\mathrm{W}\rangle$ leaves
\begin{equation}
  \rho_{\mathrm{pair}} = \tfrac23\,\lvert\Psi^+\rangle\langle\Psi^+\rvert
  + \tfrac13\,\lvert00\rangle\langle00\rvert,
  \qquad \lvert\Psi^+\rangle = \tfrac1{\sqrt2}(\lvert01\rangle+\lvert10\rangle),
\end{equation}
a Bell pair of weight $\tfrac23$ against a separable remainder --- in contrast to GHZ, whose
two-qubit marginals are separable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Group by the traced qubit and use $\lvert01\rangle+\lvert10\rangle = \sqrt2\,\lvert\Psi^+\rangle$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{One invariant: the Bloch radius of the cut}

One local-unitary invariant drives everything, and it is a coordinate on the Hopf base: the Bloch
radius $r$ of the split qubit's marginal --- equivalently its spectrum $\tfrac12(1\pm r)$. By
Lemma~\ref{app:lem:base} the base $S^8$ is the unit sphere of (Bloch vector of the cut) $\oplus$
(colour sector), and by Lemma~\ref{app:lem:ceiling} the $\mathbb{C}$-accessibility of an orbit is
exactly $r^2$. At $r = 0$ the orbit sits on the colour equator $S^5$ --- confinement, and a marginal
with no doublet axis; at $r = \tfrac13$ it occupies the band about that equator, with ceiling
$\tfrac19$ --- access to the lepton sector, and a distinguished $T_3$ axis. The entanglement-class
names label these loci; the content of the propositions is the loci themselves. Because reduced
spectra are local-unitary invariants, the distinction is frame-independent on the canonical orbits
to which, by the identification of \S\ref{sec:canonical-orbits}, the physical particles correspond.
